# The annotations of $EDE4-$FF25 in its plain view: the decryption chain and the SpeedLock loader, the source of
# src/loader/chain.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py --part src/loader/chain.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
# The plain view is each byte after the last layer that rewrites it (tools/speedlock.py plain); tools/speedlock.py
# tape turns it back into the tape's bytes, identical (tools/build.sh). The layer entries come from
# work/stage2/loader/layer_ann.py (a run of the chain in SkoolKit's simulator over the plain view).
# Witnesses: the chain run (work/parts/chain-trace.json: every instruction of the 103 layers); c1 (work/stage1/c1, a
# 48K from power-on, the tape at signal speed) for the loader $FBD4-$FF25.

@equ $0000 RomStart at $FBDA,$FBDF,$FBE4 ; the ROM's first byte, $F3 (DI) in every Spectrum ROM
@equ $006D RomModelByte ; a ROM byte: $28 in the +2A/+3 ROM (TimeCheck skips its compare there)
@equ $033A RomCrash ; the middle of the ROM's JP M,$034F at $0339: a jump there crashes the machine
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the bitmap: the countdown's digits at $5092 on
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the attributes
@equ $5D00 ChainRam 2 ; the bottom of the memory LoaderMain clears
@equ $8000 AttrBuffer 768 ; the loading screen's attributes as the first table row loads them
@equ $82FF AttrRevealOff ; the last byte of AttrBuffer: zero while the bitmap loads (Sl1Start), the attributes' last byte after
@equ $9000 TimeBuffer 255 ; the edge counts TimeCheck measures
@equ $FF26 Sl1Start ; the loader's second stage (src/loader/sl1.asm), tape block 7
@equ $FF49 BlockTable ; the block table, five bytes a row (src/loader/sl1.asm)

@module chain ; The decryption chain: 103 layers, each rewriting the rest and running into it
@about CopyChain (src/tape/basic.asm) returns into $EDE4. Each layer (all but a few) first shifts the 30 bytes behind its own LDDR down by one, which garbles the code already run, then rewrites every byte from its own end up to $FF25 and runs into the bytes it has just rewritten: the next layer. Four kinds of rewrite: p = key + c then a CPL, a SUB D or a SUB n (67 layers), p = key - c then an XOR (13), p = key XOR c (22; in 12 of them D, the key's second half, is the byte the layer wrote just before, plus 1) and p = c - 1 (1), where c is the byte before and p after. The keys come from the R register, which Layer000 sets to $42, from constants and from the loop counters, never from the bytes except in the 12 chained XOR layers.
@about tools/speedlock.py runs the chain in SkoolKit's simulator both ways: from the tape it gives this plain view, from this plain view it gives the tape's bytes back (identical, tools/build.sh). The last layer falls into LoaderStart $FBD4. Twelve layers whose loop ends with JP Z,next / JP loop leave a two-byte gap after the JP that no instruction runs: the LayerNNNGap entries.

$EDE4 c Layer000 ; Layer 0: rewrite the 4374 bytes $EE10-$FF25 (ADD A,(HL)), run into them
> Shifts the 30 bytes up to its own LDDR down by one (the code already run is garbled). Then rewrites each byte c of $EE10-$FF25 into p = (R XOR D) + c - $07, D = $8C (R the refresh register as LD A,R reads it, c the byte before, p after), and goes on at $EE11. Known from the code and the chain run of tools/speedlock.py (all 103 layers; the tape masters back identical, tools/build.sh).
$EE03 Layer000Loop ; one byte a pass

$EE11 c Layer001 ; Layer 1: rewrite the 4346 bytes $EE2C-$FF25 (XOR (HL)), run into them
> No LDDR shift. Then rewrites each byte c of $EE2C-$FF25 into p = (R XOR c) XOR D, D = $84 at the first byte, then the byte it wrote plus 1 (R the refresh register as LD A,R reads it, c the byte before, p after), and goes on at $EE2F. Known from the code and the chain run of tools/speedlock.py (all 103 layers; the tape masters back identical, tools/build.sh).
$EE14 Layer001Shift ; the shift and the set-up (the entry's JP lands here)
$EE1C Layer001Loop ; one byte a pass

$EE2D b Layer001Gap ; 2 bytes inside layer 1 no instruction runs (DD E9)
> Not executed in the chain run (SkoolKit's simulator, every instruction of the 103 layers): the layer's loop jumps over them. Kept as bytes of the block; the layers before rewrite them like their neighbours. Known only from the code ($EE2D).

$EE2F c Layer002 ; Layer 2: rewrite the 4316 bytes $EE4A-$FF25 (ADD A,(HL)), run into them
> Shifts the 30 bytes up to its own LDDR down by one (the code already run is garbled). Then rewrites each byte c of $EE4A-$FF25 into p = R + c (R the refresh register as LD A,R reads it, c the byte before, p after), and goes on at $EE4B. Known from the code and the chain run of tools/speedlock.py (all 103 layers; the tape masters back identical, tools/build.sh).
$EE40 Layer002Loop ; one byte a pass

