@module loader ; The SpeedLock loader: the hardware check, the memory clear, the second stage, the timing check
@about The last layer falls into LoaderStart. It checks for a Multiface-style device paged over the ROM (IN from ports $3F and $9F, then the ROM's first byte must still be $F3), sends 0 or 1 to ports $1F, $7B and $BB, wipes $0000-$3FFF (a RAM paged there is zeroed; the ROM ignores it) and runs LoaderMain. LoaderMain clears the chain's memory, loads the second stage (tape block 7) to $FF26 through TimeCheck, clears the screen and falls into it.
@about c1: LoaderStart runs once; the second stage loads; TimeCheck's comparison passes (FailFlag stays 0). docs/03-build-and-load.md has the tape's blocks.

$FBD4 c LoaderStart ; Check for a Multiface, page out the add-ons, wipe $0000-$3FFF, then LoaderMain
> DI; SP = $FFFF. IN A,($3F) and IN A,($9F) (the reads that page a Multiface in); after each, the ROM's first byte must be $F3, else JP RomStart (a reset). OUT ($1F),0, OUT ($7B),1, OUT ($BB),1 (none of them is the 128K's $7FFD: bit 1 of every port is set). (0) := 0 and LDIR $0000 -> $0001 for 16383 bytes; OUT ($7F),$43; LDDR $3FFE -> $3FFF. With the ROM in place these writes do nothing; a RAM over $0000 (a Multiface's, a ROM switcher's) is wiped. JP LoaderMain. What the ports drive is known only from the code ($FBD8-$FC01): c1 has no add-on, the checks pass.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$FBDF LoaderReset ; JP RomStart: the device answered over the ROM
$FBFE ; LDIR: c1 16383 passes
$FC08 ; LDDR: c1 16383 passes

@module reveal ; The edge loop's task: the attribute reveal, then the countdown
@about LoadStream's bit loop calls the routine whose address is the operand of the CALL at $FDCF (BitHook) once a bit, between the two edges of the bit. It works in the alternate registers and ends by jumping to EdgeWait, so its time is part of the bit's timing window. Three tasks follow each other: AttrRevealStart pads the time while AttrRevealOff is 0 (the bitmap loads), then (with the attributes loaded) sets up AttrReveal, which copies 12 attribute bytes a call from AttrBuffer to the screen, 64 calls; then CountdownTask draws and counts down the four digits at character row 20, columns 13-18.

$FC0D c AttrRevealStart ; The first task: wait while AttrRevealOff is 0, then point BitHook at AttrReveal
> Entered from BitHook (and from the second stage's original end on a failure). AttrRevealOff = 0: pad the time (DJNZ of 12) and go to EdgeWait. Else BitHook := AttrReveal, HL' = AttrBuffer, DE' = Attributes, A' = $41 (64 calls and the switch), and go on through the same delay.
> Witness: L1 56665 (c1).
$FC28 AttrRevealDelay ; DJNZ: the time a call takes
$FC2F AttrRevealPad ; AttrRevealOff = 0: one NOP more, the same time as the set-up
$FC32 AttrReveal ; The second task: copy 12 attribute bytes from AttrBuffer to the screen; after 64 calls, CountdownStart
$FC55 CountdownStart ; BitHook := CountdownTask; border mask 0 and OR 8 at EdgeBorderMask (the border black from now)
$FC6D CountdownStartDelay ; DJNZ: the time

$FC73 c CountdownTask ; The third task: draw the four countdown digits, count the time down, decrement the counter
> A' is the phase. $9C-$98: draw the digits, one glyph's 4 rows a call (C' calls a digit), from CountdownDigits (src/loader/sl1.asm, $FF80 + 8 x digit, the glyph pointers at CountdownCounter) to the screen: the last digit at $5092, the others 2, 3 and 5 columns left of it; below $98 A' counts the calls down to 0, each padded to the same time; at 0, A' := $9C and the counter goes down by one: the last two digits wrap from 0 to 9, the second from 0 to 5 ($FCD9-$FD00). It starts at 0 2 1 1 (pointers $80 $90 $88 $88). c1: the counter moved 212 times.
> Witness: L1 34614 (c1).
$FC7F CountdownRow ; one glyph row to the screen, 4 rows
$FC8A CountdownNextDigit ; the next digit: its pointer and screen cell from A'
$FCB6 CountdownDigitDelay ; DJNZ: the same time for each digit
$FCC0 CountdownPad ; the calls between two draws: only the time
$FCC2 CountdownPadSwap
$FCC3 CountdownPadDelay
$FCC9 CountdownPadLong
$FCCD CountdownTick ; a digit that did not wrap: store it
$FCCE CountdownTickDelay ; DJNZ: the same time whatever wrapped
$FCD2 CountdownTickPad
$FCD6 CountdownPhase ; A' below $98: count it down; at 0 the counter ticks
$FD00 CountdownStore ; the last digit touched, then C' := 1 and EdgeWait

$FD07 b CountdownCounter 4 ; The four digits as pointers' low bytes into CountdownDigits ($80 = 0, $C8 = 9)
> Read and written by CountdownTask (c1: 212 decrements). Initially 0 2 1 1.

@module reader ; The block reader: edges, pilot, sync, bytes, the block table, the end check
@about LoadStream reads a stream of blocks: a pilot of its own timing (32 edge pairs, then the sync pulse), seven timed edge pairs (EdgeWindows), a 6-bit mark that must be $3A, then the first block's bytes to IX, DE+1 of them, each byte stored as (L XOR $C1) + $11 by StoreByte (operands rewritten by tape blocks 19 and 21 to the real keys) and XORed into H. With LastStoreJump pointed at StoreLast (by the second stage) the last byte of a block is stored by StoreLast, which then walks BlockTable: each row pages $7FFD, takes the next block's address and length, and reads it after a 21-bit mark. An end row reads 14 more bytes and the check byte; LoadEnd wants H = 0.
@about The bit timing: EdgeDelay waits A x 16 T, Edge counts the time to the next edge in B (from the value it is given; a timeout when B wraps), and a bit is 1 when B passed the threshold. Each edge sets the border from the tape level (C), masked by EdgeBorderMask: red and black at the pilot, blue and black for the data, black once the countdown starts.

$FD0B c EdgePair ; Wait for two edges; carry clear on a timeout
> Inputs: B the start count. Output: B the count at the second edge, carry set when both came in time. Witness: L1 78 (c1).
$FD0E ; the first edge timed out: return with carry clear

$FD0F c Edge ; Wait about 350 T (A = $16), then for the next edge
> Witness: L1 3117 (c1).
$FD11 EdgeDelay ; A x 16 T of delay, then EdgeWait
$FD14 EdgeWait ; carry clear, then poll the EAR bit (port $7FFE bit 6) until it changes, counting B up; B wrapping to 0 is a timeout (carry clear)
$FD15 EdgeWaitLoop ; one poll
$FD24 EdgeBorderMask ; AND n / OR n: the border colour from C (n rewritten: 2 at the pilot, 1 for the data, 0 at the countdown; the OR 8 turns into 8)

$FD2C c LoadStream ; Read the pilot, the sync and the timing pairs, then the first block to IX, DE+1 bytes, and the rest
> Inputs: IX the first block's address, DE its length less one; LastStoreJump where its last byte goes (LoadEnd at first, StoreLast once the second stage set it). DI; border mask 2; pushes LoadFail, so a timeout's RET NC ends the stream with carry clear; C := the EAR level. The pilot: 32 edge pairs each in the window B from $9C to above $C2; then edges until a short pulse (B under $D4); then the seven pairs of EdgeWindows; then a 6-bit mark (L := 4 shifted left) that must be $3A, else JP RomCrash. H := 0 (the XOR of the bytes), IY := BlockTable, and the bit loop. Output: carry set and H = 0 on a good stream (LoadEnd). Side effects: the blocks in memory, $7FFD paged by the table rows, the border.
> Witness: L1 2 (c1).
$FD41 LoadPilotRestart ; a pair out of its window: start the pilot again
$FD43 LoadPilotPair
$FD52 LoadSync ; edges until the short pulse
$FD63 LoadTimingPairs ; the seven pairs of EdgeWindows, each between its two bounds
$FD68 LoadTimingPair
$FD77 LoadMark ; the 6-bit mark: border mask 1, L := 4
$FD85 LoadMarkBit
$FD87 LoadMarkBitWait
$FD9F ; the mark must be $3A
$FDA2 ; else JP RomCrash: a crash, no message
$FDA5 ; LD H,$86 then LD H,0: H the XOR of the bytes, from 0

$FDB5 c StoreByte ; Store a byte: (L XOR k) + a to (IX), DE down by one, then read the next byte
> k ($FDB6) and a ($FDB9) are the operands of this code: block 19 (the table's row 2, src/tape/tape.txt) rewrites them. Witness: L1 11405 (c1).
$FDC9 BitNext ; the next bit: A = 9 for EdgeDelay
$FDCB BitRead ; one bit: EdgeDelay with A, then the task, then the threshold
$FDCF BitHook ; CALL the task (the operand: AttrRevealStart, AttrReveal, CountdownTask): it ends at EdgeWait, the second edge
$FDD5 BitThreshold ; a bit is 1 when B passed $D0
$FDDF ByteDone ; 8 bits: H := H XOR L; more bytes: StoreByte
$FDE6 LastStoreJump ; JP n: where a block's last byte goes (LoadEnd, then StoreLast)

$FDE9 c StoreLast ; Store a block's last byte with its own key, then the next table row
> Its operands ($FDEA and $FDED) are rewritten by block 21 (the table's row 3). Then the 7-bit mark after the block (ReadMark, L := 2); then the table row at IY: its $7FFD value (0 ends the table), its address to IX, a 21-bit mark whose middle 7 bits must be $7F (else FailFlag := $7F: the load goes on and fails at the end), its length less one to DE, IY moves 5 on, the last mark and BitRead. A length of 0 (the end row) puts LastStoreJump back to LoadEnd and reads 14 bytes and the check byte at IX (tape block 35, at $5B21). Witness: L1 11 (c1).
$FDFD NextTableRow ; the row's $7FFD value; 0: the end
$FE19 TableRowMark ; the 21-bit mark's middle part
$FE29 TableRowLength ; DE := the row's length less one, IY := the next row
$FE4B TableEnd ; the end row: the last 14 bytes and the check, then LoadEnd
$FE5A TableEndRow ; a zero $7FFD value: no OUT
$FE5E ReadMarkBit ; A = 12 for the next bit

$FE60 c ReadMark ; Read the bits of a mark into L until its marker bit shifts out
> Inputs: L the marker (1 or 2, so 7 or 6 bits), A the first delay, B the start count. Output: L the bits, carry set; carry clear on a timeout. Witness: L1 231 (c1).

@module timing ; The timing check: load the second stage, sample the tape's edges, compare the two ends
@about TimeCheck is LoaderMain's call: it loads tape block 7 and then measures how often the EAR bit changes in 255 windows; a tape played too fast or too slow (a copy, a turbo deck) sets FailFlag, which Sl1End (src/loader/sl1end.asm) turns into a crash at the end of the load.

$FE76 c TimeCheck ; Load the second stage, then measure the tape's edges into TimeBuffer and compare them
> CALL LoadStream (IX = Sl1Start, DE = $AA: tape block 7). Then 255 TimeSample counts to TimeBuffer. On the +2A/+3 ROM (its byte $006D is $28) no compare; else TimeCompare. Tape block 29 loads Unxor over this routine later (src/loader/unxor.asm). Witness: L1 2 (c1).
$FE7E TimeCheckLoop ; one sample: c1 255 passes

$FE91 c TimeSample ; Count the changes of EAR (port $7FFE bit 6) in 255 polls into E
> Witness: L1 255 (c1).
$FE96 TimeSamplePoll
$FEA5 TimeSampleNext
$FEA8 TimeSampleSame ; no change: the same time as a change

$FEAD c TimeCompare ; S1 = samples 50-99, S2 = samples 205-254; FailFlag := H of S2 when it is $0D or more, 1 when S2 - S1 >= 50
> A tape played at the right speed gives about the same counts at both ends of the block. Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$FEB5 TimeSumFirst
$FEC8 TimeSumLast
$FEE5 TimeFailed ; FailFlag := A

$FEE9 b FailFlag ; Non-zero when the timing check failed (1 or S2's high byte) or a table mark was wrong ($7F)
> Read by Sl1End (src/loader/sl1end.asm) and by the second stage's original end. c1: 0 at the end of the load.

@module reader ; The block reader: edges, pilot, sync, bytes, the block table, the end check

$FEEA b EdgeWindows 2 ; The seven timed pairs after the sync: the start count and the bound, a pair a row
> Read by LoadStream at $FD68 (c1). ($E9,$EA), ($D2,$E2), ($B6,$E0) three times, ($D2,$E2), ($EC,$ED).

$FEF8 c LoadEnd ; The stream's end: border black, H (the XOR of the bytes) must be 0, else RomCrash
> Reached through LastStoreJump with the pushed LoadFail on the stack (POP DE drops it), or by a timeout's RET NC straight into LoadFail. Output: carry set when H = 0. Witness: L1 2 (c1).
$FEF9 LoadFail ; a timeout comes here with carry clear: border black, and H decides as well

@module loader ; The SpeedLock loader: the hardware check, the memory clear, the second stage, the timing check

$FF03 c LoaderMain ; Clear $5D00-$FC0C, load the second stage at $FF26, clear the screen and fall into it
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$FF19 ; clear $4000-$5AFF: the screen, black
$FF22 ; then into Sl1Start at $FF26
